.Dd October 19, 2026
.Dt ESPI_FLASH 4D
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm espi_flash
.Nd eSPI flash access channel driver
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /devices/kunlun@0,0/espi@1/espi_flash@3:flash
.Pp
.In sys/espi_flash.h
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
driver provides access to a flash device that is attached to an eSPI target
and reached over the flash access channel of an eSPI controller.
It attaches as a child of
.Xr espi 4D
when the controller's target has flash attached to it and the channel has been
enabled.
.Pp
The device is a character special file.
.Xr read 2 ,
.Xr write 2 ,
.Xr pread 2
and
.Xr pwrite 2
transfer data at the file offset, which is the address within the flash.
Transfers of any size and alignment are accepted and are divided by the driver
into the requests that the eSPI link allows.
.Pp
The driver does not erase the flash before writing to it.
As is usual for NOR flash, the blocks to be written must first be erased with
the
.Dv ESPI_FLASH_IOC_ERASE
ioctl, and the data read back afterwards to verify it.
The target may not allow writes or erases, for example until the service
processor permits them, in which case the operation fails with
.Er EIO .
.Pp
The size of the flash is not reported by eSPI.
When the platform supplies it, reads stop at the end of the flash, writes that
start at or beyond it fail with
.Er ENOSPC ,
and the device registers with the
.Xr ufm 4D
framework so that its contents can be read with tools such as
.Xr fwflash 8 .
When the size is not known the whole 32-bit eSPI flash address space is
accessible and no UFM image is registered.
.Pp
The device may be opened with
.Dv O_EXCL
to prevent other opens while the flash is being reprogrammed.
.Sh IOCTLS
The ioctls are defined in
.In sys/espi_flash.h .
.Bl -tag -width Dv
.It Dv ESPI_FLASH_IOC_INFO
Describes the device.
The argument is a pointer to an
.Vt espi_flash_info_t
which is filled in by the driver:
.Bd -literal
typedef struct espi_flash_info {
	uint64_t	efi_size;
	uint32_t	efi_payload;
	uint32_t	efi_maxread;
	uint32_t	efi_erase_sizes;
	uint32_t	efi_pad;
} espi_flash_info_t;
.Ed
.Pp
.Fa efi_size
is the size of the flash in bytes, or 0 if it is not known.
.Fa efi_payload
and
.Fa efi_maxread
are the largest number of bytes that a single write request and a single read
request on the eSPI link may carry.
Transfers that are multiples of these sizes and aligned to them are the most
efficient.
.Fa efi_erase_sizes
has bit
.Em n
set if the target has advertised that blocks of 2^n KiB can be erased.
If it is 0 the target advertised no erase block sizes and the driver accepts
any that the eSPI protocol can request.
.It Dv ESPI_FLASH_IOC_ERASE
Erases one block of the flash.
The file descriptor must be open for writing.
The argument is a pointer to an
.Vt espi_flash_erase_t :
.Bd -literal
typedef struct espi_flash_erase {
	uint64_t	efe_offset;
	uint64_t	efe_length;
} espi_flash_erase_t;
.Ed
.Pp
.Fa efe_length
is the size of the block and must be one of 4 KiB, 32 KiB, 64 KiB or 128 KiB,
which are the sizes that the eSPI protocol can request, and, if the target
advertised erase block sizes, one of those.
.Fa efe_offset
is the address of the block and must be a multiple of
.Fa efe_length .
.El
.Sh ARCHITECTURE
The
.Nm
driver is only available on the oxide platform.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Pa
.It Pa /platform/oxide/kernel/drv/amd64/espi_flash
64-bit AMD64 ELF kernel driver
.El
.Sh ERRORS
On failure,
.Va errno
is set to indicate the error.
The more common errors are listed below.
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Er EBADF
.Dv ESPI_FLASH_IOC_ERASE
was requested on a file descriptor that is not open for writing.
.It Er EBUSY
The device is already open and
.Dv O_EXCL
was requested, or it is open with
.Dv O_EXCL ,
or the eSPI controller could not accept a request.
.It Er EFAULT
The driver could not copy data to or from the address space of the calling
process.
.It Er EINVAL
The erase block size is not one that can be requested, the block is not
aligned to its size, or it lies outside the flash.
.It Er EIO
The target reported that it could not complete a request, or the data it
returned did not match what was asked for.
.It Er ENOSPC
A write starts at or beyond the end of the flash.
.It Er ENOTSUP
The erase block size is not one that the target advertised.
.It Er ENXIO
The device does not exist or the flash access channel is not enabled.
.It Er ETIMEDOUT
The target did not complete a request in time.
.El
.Sh INTERFACE STABILITY
.Sy Uncommitted
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ioctl 2 ,
.Xr open 2 ,
.Xr pread 2 ,
.Xr pwrite 2 ,
.Xr read 2 ,
.Xr write 2 ,
.Xr espi 4D ,
.Xr ufm 4D ,
.Xr fwflash 8
